<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tercio</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The tercio was a Spanish infantry formation of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries that combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>, swordsmen and arquebusiers in large, disciplined squares. It made the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> among the most formidable in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> reformed this model by breaking the tercio's massive squares into smaller, more manoeuvrable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> and pikemen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tercio.jpg" alt="Tercio" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Habsburgs Castilian infantry formed the core of the <b>tercios</b> that fought in Italy and Flanders, while Castilian conquistadors such as Hernán Cortés and Francisco Pizarro overthrew the Aztec and Inca empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain’s infantry, organised from the 1530s in the famous <b>tercios</b> of pikemen and musketeers, was regarded as the best in Europe for more than a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish infantry, victorious at Pavia (1525) and organised from 1534 into <b>tercios</b> combining pikemen and arquebusiers, gave Spain military dominance over its European opponents for more than a century, a reputation that was badly dented by defeat at Rocroi in 1643.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Building on Dutch practice, Gustavus replaced the massive infantry squares of the Spanish <b>tercio</b> with smaller, more manoeuvrable brigades of musketeers and pikemen; introduced light regimental guns that could be moved and fired on the battlefield rather than used mainly for sieges; and employed cavalry aggressively in shock…</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat led to Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba's creation of the <b>tercios</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat of the famed <b>tercios</b> signalled the decline of Spanish power on land.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
